printf - print, with control over exactly what comes out

SYNOPSIS
    printf FORMAT [ARGUMENT...]

DESCRIPTION
    Writes the format, with % conversions replaced by the arguments and
    backslash escapes expanded. Unlike echo it adds no newline of its
    own, which is the point: what comes out is what was asked for.

    Escapes: \n \t \r \a \b \f \v \\ \e, \xNN and \0NNN.
    \e is byte 27, which is how a script emits an escape sequence -
    `printf '\e[31mred\e[0m\n'`.

    Conversions: %s %d %i %u %x %X %o %c %f %e %g and %%, each taking the
    next argument, with the usual flags, width and precision. %b is the
    argument with its own escapes expanded.

    The format is reused until the arguments run out, so
    `printf '%s\n' a b c` prints three lines. A format with no
    conversions in it is printed once.

SEE ALSO
    echo, sh, tee
